<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Putins War The Bodo Express, 2022</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY text=#dcdcdc bgColor=#2b2b2b scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;CMO: Putin’s War: The Bodo Express, 2022&lt;BR&gt;Al “Beirutdude” Sandrik&lt;BR&gt;The Time Is: 27 June, 2022 17:00:00 Zulu/18:00:00 Oslo Time&lt;BR&gt;AOR: Scandinavia and North Sea &lt;BR&gt;Playable as NATO or Russia&lt;BR&gt;2 Days Duration&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;The Baltic Crisis finally erupted into a full-scale NATO-Russian/Belorussia conflict on the morning of June 6th. The Baltics and the Suwalki Gap were rapidly overrun by the evening of the 7th with Russian forces reaching Kaliningrad by advancing west along Highway A16/E28 from Vilnius to Kybartai. They then turned southwestward reaching Highway 63 in Poland on the 11th, where they paused.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;On the morning of the 7th Russian air forces bombed the Norwegian expeditionary air base and port at Banak, near the Nord Kapp. By afternoon Russian paratroopers had seized the air field with Northern Fleet’s 61st Naval Infantry Brigade conducting a follow up landing at the port that evening. The 200th Separate Motor Rifle Brigade crossed the Norway-Russian border that morning brushing aside the tripwire Norwegian outposts. More follow up forces from 80th Independent Motor Rifle Brigade follow on advancing west.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;A brief, but pyrrhic, naval battle broke out between Royal Norwegian Navy and Russian missile boats and naval fighter bombers based near Pechenga. While many of the Russian boats were sunk or disabled, the four Norwegian Skjold-class corvettes also died valiantly. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;The Norwegian Brigade Nord, reinforced by territorial units and the USMC’s 22nd Marine Expeditionary Unit (MEU)/Battalion Landing Team (BLT) 2/6 (deployed to the Setermoen Military Camp for Arctic Training at the onset of the battle) staged a fighting withdrawal across Finnmark. They eventually established a defensive line (Forward Edge if the Battle Area (FEBA)) along the Rossaelva River on the 14th.&amp;nbsp; By June 16th the 26th MEU/BLT 2/8 completed its air lift from Cherry Point, North Carolina to marry up with the prepositioned USMC equipment in Norway and further reinforced the new defensive line. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;To get at the Norwegian Air Force bases near Narvik, Tromso and Bodo the Russian Air Force decided to risk transiting over northern Finland and Sweden, it was mistake. Both nations decided to resist the overstretched Russian air forces, while trying to avoid ground combat. So now even, though Swedish and Finnish Air Forces/Radars were not fully integrated into NATOs air defense, the former neutrals became combatants and would have to be dealt with in the coming battle. While both nations are (somewhat) friendly to NATO (neutral game wise), allowing NATO aircraft to overfly their territory “cooperatively” but both are not fully integrated into the NATO defense structure. Thus their units are not under NATO control, nor does NATO see their radars but NATO is active over their territory.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;Over the first two weeks of the battle NATO was largely out of position with the larger surface combatants remaining south of the Greenland-Iceland-United Kingdom (GIUK) Gap to set up a screen for a Russian naval threat that wouldn’t materialize. The Russian Northern Fleet, absent the Navy Flag Ship Admiral Kuznetsov (now permanently disabled due to the sinking of the PD-19 Dry Dock and the December 2019 fire) chose to only contest the Norwegian Sea, and remained north of the GIUK Gap. That didn’t mean they weren’t active and over the first two weeks of the conflict the Royal Norwegian Navy was hunted down and sunk piece by piece. By the 19th only one Norwegian submarine, operating out of Tromso, was active with the remaining vessels being repaired either near Oslo or in UK ports. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;Conversely, NATO was also active. Recognizing the need to further reinforce Brigade Nord and Marine battalions defending the river line. The USN and USMC quickly loaded two Amphibious Ready Groups (ARG) for immediate deployment. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;The first ARG (TF 22.1) was built up around the Mayport based USS Iwo Jima (LHD-7) and included the Maritime Prepositioned Ship (MPS), USNS 1st Lt. Jack Lummus (T-AK-3011) which was just completing rehab of its stocked equipment at the USMC Support Facility, Blount Island (Jacksonville Florida, by the way, USMC Blount Island is one of the USMC’s best kept secrets, probably 90% of the population of Jacksonville doesn’t even know it is there!). The Iwo Jima’s ARG loaded the 24th MEU/BLT 1/8 at Morehead City, North Carolina on the 14th.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;BR&gt;The Second ARG (TF 22.2) was built around the Norfolk based USS Bataan (LHD-5) and loaded the 28th MEU/BLT 3/8 and additional elements of the 11th Marine Regiment (Artillery). Both departed immediately, rendezvousing south of the GIUK Gap on the 26th. Their mission was simple, sail to Norway and unload their MEUs and associated Medium Tiltrotor Squadrons (VMM) to Bodo and then exit the area as quickly as practical. Follow up convoys, with additional replenishment supplies, were departing Philadelphia and Savannah and would need the anchorage area in the coming days. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;The ARGs would not be alone. With American carriers hard pressed fighting Iran and near Syria the Royal Navy (RN) would support the reinforcement mission with a carrier battle group built around their new HMS Queen Elizabeth (R08 - V/STOL) carrier. Two composite squadrons of F-35 Lightning IIs were deployed along with supporting AEW/ASW helicopters. Two RN SSNs and one USN SSN would also support the operation.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;On the other side of the hill, the Northern Fleet threw everything into stopping the Marines arrival. Recognizing the northern flank could be lost if the reinforcements arrived every Northern Fleet surface vessel and submarine available was thrown into the fray. Naval Aviation was instructed to ignore losses and if necessary, to transit Scandinavia (Sweden and Finland) to allow the bombers to get at the amphibs. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;For both sides the fate of the northern front rested on the coming battle. The current pause has allowed for rest and reconstitution of some of the units. The Norwegian Air Force 318 Squadron was refitted with some of the remaining F-16 MLUs turning over their remaining F-35s to the two other F-35 squadrons (331 &amp;amp; 332). Airfields were repaired and, as much as possible, ammo/PGMs resupplied.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Notes:&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=red&gt;Surface ships with missions teleport, please run the scenario for 5 seconds before assigning missions. &lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;1.&lt;/STRONG&gt; Sweden and Finland are neutral to NATO only in that they are not integrated into the NATO defense structure well enough to integrate their radars. Therefore, you can overfly both nations as NATO. Russia can only overfly the northern part of both nations (as a belligerent) with the south belonging to the Western (Baltic) Military District. As such the south has a No-Fly Zone set up to avoid potential conflicts with their operations (an keep the bombers from flying too far south for the scenario).&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;2.&lt;/STRONG&gt; Russian MPA aircraft, have their own “side” and are friendly to Russia &lt;U&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;EM&gt;BUT&lt;/EM&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/U&gt; you don’t control them. I had to separate them out to build a no-fly zone for them over Scandinavia which wasn’t desirable for the other Russian air units. Let’s face it, can you see an IL-38 May overflying a SAM site near Banak or deliberately flying near F-35s! Personally, I can’t but that is exactly what the A/I will do, so, this had to be done to give the MPA A/I a fighting chance! Sorry if you enjoy “mowing the lawn” with ASW/MPA aircraft. &lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;3.&lt;/STRONG&gt; I didn’t see any sense in really getting into major cargo operations! So, unloading the amphibs is simulated by getting the NATO amphibs into the Bodo Box and remaining there for 12 hours. Please do not delete the five (5) Bodo Anchorage Box Reference Points (locked) or you lose the VPs (like 10,000 possible!). Also, be careful that you don’t allow ships in your formation to stay out of the box. The center unit may be in the box but flanking units could stray outside.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;4.&lt;/STRONG&gt; As this scenario is designed to be played from both sides feel free to delete all the other reference points (other than the locked Bodo Box for NATO) as the A/I’s missions are set to not appear for the human player. I did indicate FEBA with some locked Reference Points (for both sides), once you know where it is, go ahead and unlock/delete them to remove more clutter if you want, they are just informational. If you want to look at my missions just go into the editor and unclick the “Scrub Mission” box in the lower left hand corner for each mission and save it.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;5.&lt;/STRONG&gt; I did set initial aircraft load outs for aircraft (had to for the A/I). You’ll have plenty of time to reset them for all but the Backfires/Blackjacks (Russian player) before you need to get into operations. Loadout make up is pretty strict at the airfields. Remember the war is two weeks old, but I tried to make sure all aircraft have two (2) loads outs at their bases (again for the A/I). You can move aircraft around but will likely run low on supplies/load outs.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;6.&lt;/STRONG&gt; USN Burke Class DDGs, I adjusted the VLS missile load outs to emphasize SMs over TLAMs.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;7.&lt;/STRONG&gt; Unlike a lot of my other scenarios you don’t receive points for destroying everything in sight! The goal is to get to the anchorage for NATO and keep NATO out of it for Russia. That said, the vessels are high value units with big crews and political ramifications if damaged/sunk! Therefore, destroyed (and damaging some) ships/subs will gain you VPs, BUT for NATO you have to get to, and remain in, Bodo to win!!!!&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;8.&lt;/STRONG&gt; &lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT color=red&gt;Please turn on/allow message pop ups.&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;9. The longer the scenario goes on the greater the chance of nuclear war breaking out.&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Originally created under CMO Version 1.01 Build 1134.1 – Data Base: DB3000 483&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Updated to &lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;CMO Version 1.01 Build 1143.1 – Data Base: DB3000 483 - 4/25/2020&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P align=left&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;An .INI and Delta File have both been provided for rebuilds&lt;BR&gt;Al “BeirutDude” Sandrik (ex-Beachinnole)&lt;BR&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;A href="mailto:Al.Sandrik@gmail.com"&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;Al.Sandrik@gmail.com&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/A&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
